Topi Linkala wrote:
> Hello to you all,
> 
> first thing first; we should try to define our terms. These are my 
> definitions:
> 
> Cheat: Going outside of the game to try to do what ever one wants to 
> doby using mechanisms like: copying save files, creating wizmode bones, 
> patching and compiling and changing the existing game mechanism etc.
> 
> Abuse: Using exsiting bug that has been reported to try to circumvent 
> what the devteam has tried to mean how the game should be played.
> 
> Conduct: Not using a game mechanism that has been coded in the game by 
> the devteam.

These terms (well, cheat and abuse anyway) are not unique to Nethack. 
They should probably mean something very similar to what they mean in
other games.

Your definition of a cheat is good.  I think this is what most people
consider cheating.  Very many people would also call some forms of bug
abuse cheating, but everyone calls it abuse, so that is the better
term.

Your definition of abuse would not fly at all in other games, however.
 Some of the most notorious abuses are teamhopping and TKing,
especially in FPS games.

In many games, you can change teams at will.  This is a desired part
of the game.  However, some people will use this feature to sabotage
one team, then switch to the other team.  This is certainly behavior
that should not be done.  However, you can't code it out without
giving up a good part of the game.  The game can't read your mind, but
to give the game the ideal behavior, it would have to do just that.

TKing may be an even better example.  Friendly fire is a desired game
element in many games.  But players will abuse this system to sabatoge
and annoy other players in a way not intended by the game.  Some games
remove FF entirely to avoid this.  Others use penalty systems.  These
choices themselves make the game less like the desired game, but they
are adopted as the lesser evil.

So abuse is not limited to bugs.  Not all abuses can be coded out
without a cost.  Programs can not do absolutely anything; they are
especially limited by the available interface devices.

When people write games, they generally have an intent for how the
game will work.  The game seldom fully lives up to the intent. 
Partially because of bugs, which can and should be removed.  Partially
because of design trade offs that have to be made, and these can't be
removed.  Abusing the second is still abuse.

That doesn't make it a big deal.  In multi-player competitive games,
it usually is a big deal.  In a single player game, it pretty much
comes down to how much fun you have.  Some single-player games even
throw in cheat codes, and if you enjoy the game better with cheat
codes, then use them.  If you enjoy the game while abusing features,
then do it.

